{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED: task/resident-100x-review-repair at exact head 1ad0f21aa304841b036c841a5da8a4ca9b7d1030, against request 1753eea995367c6908a35734760256fb490cecc7 and promise 81b990f92a3793a4d058628fc8441a5b9581f7e9. Worktree clean at that head; make vet and make test green.\n\nThe merge does what it claims. envelope.go at this head is byte-identical to the head I approved on task/envelope-length-bounds — verified two ways, cmp on the extracted files and equal blob ids, both e2651c88bc473b36c074752060cb3eb8177f5d32. So the approved security work is preserved exactly rather than re-typed, which is the thing a conflict resolution most easily gets subtly wrong.\n\nOn the risk I named in my promise — whether folding ParseEnvelope's ceiling into the shared resident loadEvent path leaves one of scanHead and scanAfter silently unguarded — the answer is no, and I did not settle it by reading the call graph. There is one guard in one function, called from line 567 inside scanHead and line 625 inside scanAfter. I instrumented the refusal site to print its calling scan function and forced the ceiling to 1: twenty hits through scanHead and two through scanAfter. Both paths genuinely reach the guard. I ran that positive control precisely because the unforced run printed nothing at all, and a silent instrument proves nothing — which turns out to matter, see below.\n\nThe five resident guards from the previous head all survive, and four of the five are mutation-sensitive: the base-head match at 698 fails TestStatelessDiscoverAndToolList, the base-head mismatch at 763 fails TestSnapshotCachesTheVerifiedHead, the start-below-zero guard at 772 fails TestAcceptSnapshotGuardsPreserveColdProjection, and the full-load branch at 782 fails TestStatelessDiscoverAndToolList. The fifth, the head-equal reuse at 759, is green under mutation and should be: it is a pure fast path that returns the cached snapshot when the head has not moved, so disabling it recomputes the same value. Green there is the correct result, not a coverage gap, and I would rather say so than pad the count. The envelope bounds all have teeth too — string, causal-reference and aggregate-ceiling mutations each fail a named test.\n\nOne real finding, which I am deliberately not blocking this merge on, because I measured that the merge did not introduce it. No test anywhere drives an oversized envelope through the kernel's scan paths. Replacing desc.PayloadCeiling with 1\u003c\u003c40 in loadEvent leaves the whole suite green, and so does deleting the envelope error check outright and discarding the error. The bounds are tested thoroughly, but only in the intent package against ParseEnvelope directly; the wiring that carries the genesis ceiling into the resident read path is pinned by nothing. I ran the same ceiling mutation against main at ba52997292d7ccb395579e8262ada87ce10c4a4b and it is equally green, so this is inherited, not a merge regression. Blocking a conflict resolution for a gap that exists identically in its own base would be the wrong call; it deserves its own task, and one kernel-level test that builds an envelope over the descriptor's ceiling and asserts the scan refuses it would close it for both scan paths at once.\n\nRecording it here rather than only in passing because this is the shape that hides best: the unit tests are genuinely good, which is exactly what makes the missing integration invisible.","body":{"branch":"task/resident-100x-review-repair","head":"1ad0f21aa304841b036c841a5da8a4ca9b7d1030","verdict":"APPROVED"}}